<%# Mirrors the structure of the stock password_reset_email in spree_emails, using
    Spree's own mailer_hero and mailer_button partials rather than hand-rolled tables.
    First version built the button by hand and got a white-on-grey box that did not read
    as clickable — the partial applies Spree's own button markup and the bulletproof
    table wrapper Outlook needs.

    Rendered inside Spree's mailer layout, which supplies the store header, footer and
    the inlined stylesheet. Deliberate: the client has a welcome design of his own, but
    has only ever sent it as a PDF, never as ERB. Inventing his markup from a picture
    would be guesswork, and this has to WORK first. Swapping his template in later
    changes nothing about the mailer or the token. %>

<%= render layout: 'spree/shared/mailer_hero' do %>
  <h1><%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.heading', store: @current_store.name) %></h1>

  <p class="greeting">
    <%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.greeting',
                name: @user.first_name.presence || @user.email) %>
  </p>

  <p><%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.body', store: @current_store.name) %></p>

  <%= render 'spree/shared/mailer_button',
             url: @reset_url,
             label: Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.cta') %>

  <p class="sub"><%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.expiry') %></p>

  <%# Every mail client eventually eats a button, and this link is the only way in.
      The raw URL is the difference between a customer who signs in and a support
      message. %>
  <p class="sub">
    <%= Spree.t('customer_mailer.welcome_email.fallback') %>
    <br>
    <span style="word-break: break-all;"><%= @reset_url %></span>
  </p>
<% end %>
